Day 259/300: Balanced Binary Tree

Problem Statement #110

Problem Statement Explanation:

Given a binary tree, determine if it is height-balanced.
A height-balanced binary tree is defined as:
A binary tree in which the left and right subtrees of every node differ in height by no more than 1.

Solution Approach:

The most efficient way to solve this problem is using a Bottom-Up recursive approach.

1. The Core Idea
Instead of calling a separate height function for every node (which would be O(n^2)), we calculate the height and check for balance simultaneously. We start from the leaf nodes and work our way up to the root.

2. The Sentinel Value (-1)
We use -1 as a special flag.
If a subtree is found to be unbalanced, it returns -1.
This value "bubbles up" the recursion stack, allowing us to short-circuit the logic and avoid unnecessary calculations once an imbalance is detected.

3. Step-by-Step Logic
Base Case: If the current node is null, return 0 (height of an empty tree).
Recurse Left: Get the height of the left subtree. If it returns -1, the tree is unbalanced.
Recurse Right: Get the height of the right subtree. If it returns -1, the tree is unbalanced.
Check Balance: If the absolute difference between leftHeight and rightHeight is greater than 1, return -1.
Return Height: Otherwise, return the height of the current node: 1+max(leftHeight,rightHeight).

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public boolean isBalanced(TreeNode root) {
        return checkHeight(root) != -1;
    }

    private int checkHeight(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;

        int leftHeight = checkHeight(node.left);
        if (leftHeight == -1) return -1;

        int rightHeight = checkHeight(node.right);
        if (rightHeight == -1) return -1;

        if (Math.abs(leftHeight - rightHeight) > 1) return -1;

        return Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight) + 1;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity:	O(n)
Space Complexity: O(h)

